": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}]}}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "questions": [{"responses": [{"id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "selected": 0, "correct": 1}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "pdf", "content": "enpe7n7le03ob10re7pdf01.pdf"}], "type": "question"}]}], "template_override": 1, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"contents": [{}], "id": 0, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"id": 1, "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n7le03ob10re2img01.jpg", "time": "0", "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Thomas: </strong>Honestly, I don't see the importance of having backup and recovery strategies. Having a copy in a cloud should be enough.</br>\n<strong>Linda: </strong>Bear in mind, that there is always a chance of failure, and big companies or businesses that have tons and tons of information need to protect themselves against a possible data loss; that's why backups are cornerstones for them.</br>\n<strong>Thomas</strong>: Well, that's true, but if they have too much data to protect, how can they even do it?</br>\n<strong>Linda: </strong> There are some strategies and programs they can use. The first is RMAN, it stands for Recovery Manager, and the second one is the User-managed backup and recovery; they have slight differences.</br></div>"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n7le03ob10re2img02.jpg", "time": "47000", "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Thomas: </strong>Which ones?</br>\n<strong>Linda: </strong> RMAN focuses on performing backups and recovering activities by using a repository of historical data. User-managed is more centered in using a mix of host operating system and SQL recovery commands. RMAN is preferred over user-managed because it has more tools, like incremental backups, block media recovery, unused block and binary compression, and encrypted backups.</br>\n<strong>Thomas</strong>: If what you are telling me is true, I'd always choose RMAN.</br></div>"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n7le03ob10re2img03.jpg", "time": "89000", "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Linda: </strong> Well, regardless of your choice, you can also create physical backups; they can even be imported. Also, in Oracle Database, you can use the Oracle Flashback technology. It doesn't depend on RMAN either; it depends upon undoing data and records when databases are updated.</br>\n<strong>Thomas</strong>: But what about a program to see if there have been data losses?</br>\n<strong>Linda: </strong> There is one, it is called Data Recovery Advisor. It diagnoses persistent data failures, and it even gives you options to do the necessary repairs; it can also execute them if you order it to do it. </br>\n<strong>Thomas</strong>: Well, if there are so many strategies and programs used to recover the data, it must be a pretty big deal.</div>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n7le03ob10re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n7le03ob10re2aud01"}}], "_data": [], "title": "", "template_override": 1, "type": "audio"}]}]}